#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060906 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the equation $\\log_3 x = 4.$ Can we determine $x?$\n\nRecall that the inverse operation of the logarithm is exponentiation. So, to isolate $x,$ we must exponentiate both sides of this equation. \n\nThe base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use a base of $3$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {3}^{({log}_{3}⁡x)} & ={3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above may look complicated, but since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $3^{(\\log_3{x})}=x.$ Therefore, we get   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={3}^{4} \\\\ x & =81.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x = 81$ is the solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "bbfe9e19-d1ed-5df9-97f9-0e9c7f9f50e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2810 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
